		<div class="popup-video__video-wrap">
	        <p class="popup-video__current-video-title hidden" tabindex="0"></p>
			
				
					
						<div class="video" data-image-default="false" data-video-embed="false" data-video-type="youtube" data-video-data='{
							"id": "2gYqEi8-am4",
							"title": "A cinematic product film showcases the Galaxy S26 Ultra, highlighting AI-driven features, pro-level cameras, a high-performance processor, and an innovative privacy display that enhance everyday usability.",
							"caption": "The video opens with a side view of the Galaxy S26 Ultra, slowly zooming in before transitioning to the Samsung logo. A sleek side profile of the device is shown as a star-shaped Galaxy icon moves swiftly along its edge, leading into the text “Galaxy AI.” The rear camera appears in close-up with the text “Next-Level Pro Cameras.” The scene then transitions inside the device to reveal its chipset, accompanied by the text “The Most Powerful Processor on Galaxy.” The camera moves outward from the chipset to the front display, where “The World’s First Privacy Display on Mobile” text appears. The phone rotates to reveal its back again, followed by the text “Introducing the New Galaxy S26 Ultra.” The scene then shifts to lifestyle footage, beginning with a woman focused on her phone in a subway station, alongside the text “The Most Powerful Processor on Galaxy.” A close-up of her screen shows a colorful, fast-paced game. At the bottom part of the video, Genshin Impact and HoYoverse logos are shown, along with the disclaimer stating, “Copyright © COGNOSPHERE. All Rights Reserved. This video has been edited for demonstration purposes. Actual usage may vary. Available for purchase in Samsung Galaxy Store and other app stores in select countries.” The view transitions into the device’s internal chipset, highlighting CPU and GPU components with text such as “Customized Processor,” “39% Faster NPU,” “24% Faster GPU,” “19% Faster CPU,” and “Image Enhancer,” along with the disclaimer stating, “Compared to the Galaxy S25 Ultra. Based on internal testing. Performance may vary depending on testing conditions and environment.” The device exterior reappears with abstract waves radiating outward, suggesting enhanced performance, along with the disclaimer stating, “Galaxy S26 Ultra compared with previous Galaxy smartphones.” The focus returns to the chipset with the text, “Customized Image Enhancer,” followed by a demonstration in which a sunset beach image becomes more vivid and detailed on the phone screen, along with the disclaimer stating, “Compared to the Galaxy S25 Ultra.” The woman notices an arriving train and stands up. Inside a crowded subway, the text, “The World’s First Privacy Display on Mobile” appears. The woman looks around as others stand close by. She activates a feature on her phone and the screen darkens when viewed from a 45-degree angle, remaining visible only when viewed directly, along with a disclaimer stating, “Available on Galaxy S26 Ultra only Privacy Display feature controls the screen&#39;s viewing angle to limit peripheral vision and can be activated in Settings. Activation for specific apps also available in Settings. Some changes in image quality may occur outside the viewing angle. Some information may still be visible to others depending on the viewing environment, such as angle or brightness. Caution is advised when exposing sensitive information.” Close-ups of pixel-level adjustments illustrate how specific areas or apps stay hidden from side angles by controlling the display’s pixels, narrowing and widening them to determine how light is directed and who can see the content. The texts, “Narrow pixel” and “Wide pixel” appear. The scene transitions to a woman using her phone in an elevator, turning off “Privacy display,” which shows a photo of a dog on-screen. As she exits, the setting shifts to a park. A hand holding the Galaxy S26 Ultra records dogs and their owner, alongside the text, “Next-Level Pro Cameras.” A close-up of the camera interface shows movement while running, yet the footage remains stable, emphasized by the text, “Super Steady Video,” with a person walking dogs appearing on-screen. A couple reviews smooth footage of dogs playing. The scene then transitions to a nighttime campsite, where a user records video in low light, highlighted by the text “Nightography Video.” The camera lenses are shown with specifications including the texts, “F1.4,” “50MP Tele 10X,” “200MP Wide,” “50MP Ultra Wide,” and “10MP Tele 3X,” with upgraded pro cameras featuring up to a 47% brighter aperture, along with a disclaimer stating, “Tele 3x is optical zoom. Tele 10x is optical-quality zoom.” A zoom into the lens reveals bright, clear footage of people cooking and enjoying a party at night. Photos captured on the device are displayed, and a man and woman review them at the campsite as the “Galaxy AI” logo appears. The phone interface then shows Photo Assist in action, typing “Add my dog” text inserts a dog into a photo, “Change to daytime” text transforms the lighting, “Birthday party in wonderland” text changes the scene to a fantasy setting, and “Add birthday sunglasses on dog” text adds accessories to the dog, along with the disclaimer stating, “Photo Assist requires a network connection and Samsung Account login. Service availability may vary by country, language or device model. Photo Assist may result in a resized photo. A visible watermark is overlaid on the image output upon saving in order to indicate that the image is generated by AI. The accuracy and reliability of the generated output is not guaranteed.” The Creative Studio feature is introduced with the text, “The creative process is in full swing…” then showing a UI where a selected photo generates multiple dog-character emojis, which are then applied as stickers on the actual picture, along with the disclaimer stating, “Creative Studio feature requires a network connection and Samsung Account login. Service availability may vary by country, language or device model. The accuracy and reliability of the generated output is not guaranteed. Sticker sets only compatible with Samsung keyboard and can only be shared via Quick Share feature with other Galaxy devices that have One UI 5.0 or later installed and Quick Share app version 13.8.50 x or later.” These emojis are also used in messaging. The scene shifts indoors, where “Now Nudge” text appears as a user types on their phone. A message suggesting “Luneyard Cafe” triggers a quick-access map. The user checks details and easily shares photos through the interface, along with the disclaimer stating, “Now Nudge feature requires a Samsung Account login. Available functions and features may vary by country, region, and language. Text message prompts available with certain third-party messaging apps including Samsung Messages, Google Messages, Google Chat, WhatsApp, WhatsApp Business, KakaoTalk, LINE, Signal, Instagram (DM), Tango, NTT Docomo Messages, and KDDI Messages. Share photos in Now Nudge works by analyzing available image content. Accuracy of results is not guaranteed.” The same user is then shown lying on a bed using the phone as “Now Brief” text appears. The interface displays a daily summary with the text “Morning brief. Start your day with this briefing,” allowing the user to scroll through their schedule, along with the disclaimer stating, “Now Brief requires Samsung Account login. Service availability may vary by country, language or device model, or apps. Some features may require a network connection. Recalled data is available from Notifications, Messages, Gmail, and Samsung Wallet. Notifications must be switched on for recalled data to be enabled. Recalled data collected from Gmail is only applicable if the user consents to Personal Data Intelligent&#39;s Gmail read permission. The description of photos provided by moments may not align with the user&#39;s intent. Events schedule notification is supported with calendar apps that utilize Android calendar database and available if Samsung Calendar is installed. Notifications for dog walking require SmartTag2 and Pet Care in SmartThings needs to be activated. SmartTag2 sold separately.” The camera zooms into the interface and then pulls back to reveal multiple Samsung device interfaces, transitioning into a security segment labeled “Samsung Knox.” A chipset appears with a shield icon, representing protection. The view zooms out to the Galaxy S26 Ultra, which rotates to show its rear camera and side profile once again. The text, “The New Galaxy S26 Ultra” and the “Galaxy AI” logo appear, followed by the Samsung logo to close the video, along with a disclaimer stating, “Samsung account login may be required for certain AI features. Availability of Galaxy AI features may vary depending on the region\/country, OS\/One UI version, device model, and phone carrier.” In addition to the disclaimers mentioned above, the following disclaimers apply: “Image simulated for illustrative purposes,” “Sequence shortened,” “Actual UI\/UX may differ,” and “Results may vary depending on light conditions and\/or shooting conditions.”",
							"autoplay": false}'>
						</div>
					
					
					
					
						
					
  <script type="application/ld+json" data-type="seo" data-object-type="VideoObject" data-id="fafff991-bb1c-4299-ba6d-c94f789327dc">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"VideoObject","uploadDate":"2026-05-13T16:27:20+0900","name":"A cinematic product film showcases the Galaxy S26 Ultra, highlighting AI-driven features, pro-level cameras, a high-performance processor, and an innovative privacy display that enhance everyday usability.","description":"The video opens with a side view of the Galaxy S26 Ultra, slowly zooming in before transitioning to the Samsung logo. A sleek side profile of the device is shown as a star-shaped Galaxy icon moves swiftly along its edge, leading into the text “Galaxy AI.” The rear camera appears in close-up with the text “Next-Level Pro Cameras.” The scene then transitions inside the device to reveal its chipset, accompanied by the text “The Most Powerful Processor on Galaxy.” The camera moves outward from the chipset to the front display, where “The World’s First Privacy Display on Mobile” text appears. The phone rotates to reveal its back again, followed by the text “Introducing the New Galaxy S26 Ultra.” The scene then shifts to lifestyle footage, beginning with a woman focused on her phone in a subway station, alongside the text “The Most Powerful Processor on Galaxy.” A close-up of her screen shows a colorful, fast-paced game. At the bottom part of the video, Genshin Impact and HoYoverse logos are shown, along with the disclaimer stating, “Copyright © COGNOSPHERE. All Rights Reserved. This video has been edited for demonstration purposes. Actual usage may vary. Available for purchase in Samsung Galaxy Store and other app stores in select countries.” The view transitions into the device’s internal chipset, highlighting CPU and GPU components with text such as “Customized Processor,” “39% Faster NPU,” “24% Faster GPU,” “19% Faster CPU,” and “Image Enhancer,” along with the disclaimer stating, “Compared to the Galaxy S25 Ultra. Based